const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Customized Medical Writing for Physicians","serviceType":"Bespoke clinical and scientific writing across publication, institutional, educational and patient-facing registers","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/customized-writing/","description":"Bespoke writing for clinicians across every register — reports, CME content, talks, rebuttals.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is customized medical writing, and how does it work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is writing for the documents that do not have a service page of their own: a departmental report, a CME module, a conference talk, a rebuttal letter, a white paper, a note to referrers, a medico-legal report. The process starts with the reader rather than with the subject. Who reads this, in what setting, with how much time, and what do they do afterwards — those four answers settle the register, the length and the structure before anything is drafted, and they are the reason the same clinical fact becomes four genuinely different sentences depending on where it lands. From there a writer qualified in your field drafts it, every factual claim is traced to a source that can be opened, a second person reads it against the brief rather than against the draft, and it comes back with the acknowledgement wording and the written permission for it attached."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you write my thesis or dissertation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. We do not write theses, dissertations or any other assessed work to be submitted as a student’s own. In England, providing, arranging or advertising such a service is a criminal offence under sections 27 and 28 of the Skills and Post-16 Education Act 2022. In Australia, providing or advertising academic cheating services is an offence under the TEQSA Act as amended in 2020, carrying penalties of up to 500 penalty units and two years’ imprisonment. The QAA defines contract cheating as a third party completing work that a student submits as their own where that input is not permitted, and university regulations prohibit it and typically allow only limited, declared editing and proofreading. What we do instead sits inside those boundaries: language editing to the extent your institution permits and declared where it requires declaration; statistical analysis and interpretation as a named collaborator; converting an already-examined chapter into a journal article, which is publication rather than assessment; and coaching on writing it yourself. Ask your supervisor or research office where your institution draws the line before you ask us, and we will ask you what they said."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is using a medical writer ghostwriting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only if it is concealed, and the concealment is the whole of the difference. Ghostwriting is undisclosed authorship: a document written by somebody whose involvement nobody is told about. Declared writing assistance is a named contribution in the acknowledgements with the funding stated. The ICMJE lists writing assistance among the activities that alone do not qualify somebody for authorship, says such contributors should be acknowledged with their specific contribution described, and advises editors to require that the corresponding author obtain written permission from everybody acknowledged — so the mechanism for doing this properly is published, it is short, and it costs one sentence. Worth noting: the phrase “medical writer” does not appear anywhere in the ICMJE Recommendations, and neither does “ghost”. The term used is writing assistance, and any page attributing those words to the ICMJE is quoting something else."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can my department head be an author on everything we publish?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not on the criteria, and this is worth facing rather than working around. The ICMJE requires all four criteria to be met, and states directly that acquisition of funding, general supervision of a research group and general administrative support do not on their own justify authorship. Criterion 4 is the one that makes it matter rather than merely irregular: an author is undertaking to be accountable for all aspects of the work, including ensuring that questions about the accuracy or integrity of any part are investigated and resolved. That is a real undertaking, and signing it for eleven papers a year you have not read is not a courtesy being extended to you. Where a head of department genuinely shaped the question and revised the work critically, they qualify easily and nobody needs to think about it. Where they did not, the honest routes are an acknowledgement, or — better — actually involving them. The qualifier at the top of this page will run any contributor through the four criteria and produce the wording either way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you adapt writing for different audiences?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By settling what the reader does after reading, which is a more useful question than who they are. A reviewer decides whether to believe the method, so they need enough method to judge and a claim proportionate to the design. A board decides whether to fund something, so they need the ask and the number in the first paragraph and the methodology in an appendix if at all. A referring colleague decides how to refer, so they need an instruction that survives being skimmed. A patient decides whether to be reassured and what to do next, so they need what will happen, in what order, at a reading level that has been measured rather than asserted. The sample above shows one finding written all four ways. What is not happening in any of them is simplification: the patient version is not the journal version with the statistics taken out, it answers a different question, and writing one and cutting it down produces something that serves neither reader."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you guarantee the work will be plagiarism-free?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, because it is not a claim anybody can keep. Nobody can guarantee an absolute of that kind, and the guarantee is worth less than the thing it replaces. What you get instead is an iThenticate similarity report with every match read and classified by a person, which is more useful in clinical writing than any percentage: staging criteria, drug names, standard operative descriptions and instrument names produce unavoidable overlap, and a raw similarity score misrepresents that badly. The question that matters is which matches are boilerplate that no reviewer will object to and which are somebody else’s sentences. We also run AI-content screening and tell you the result rather than only acting on it, because those tools have false positives and you should know what a journal might see."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you handle a whole year of a department's output?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is usually cheaper per document than commissioning them separately, for a reason that has nothing to do with volume discounts. The expensive part of medical writing is not the writing; it is establishing the facts — finding the figure, checking it against source, resolving the two places it is reported differently and deciding which is right. Done once per study, that fact set then supports the paper, the abstract, the poster, the talk, the departmental report and the patient material, and they agree with each other. Commissioned separately over eighteen months by three different people, the same verification is done three times and produces documents that disagree in small ways nobody notices until somebody does. A publication plan also settles the authorship questions once rather than eleven times, which is worth more than it sounds. Where the underlying research is company-sponsored, GPP 2022 governs the publication plan alongside the ICMJE."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do you do with complex or multidisciplinary material?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Assign a writer whose field is the one the argument turns on, and bring in a second reader from the other discipline. The failure mode in multidisciplinary writing is not that anything is wrong; it is that each section is written for its own specialists and the document as a whole has no reader. A paper that moves between a surgical technique, a health economic model and a patient-reported outcome instrument needs somebody deciding what a reader of the whole thing needs to carry from one section into the next, and that is an editorial decision rather than a subject one. Practically: one writer owns the through-line, specialists review their own sections, and the second reader is chosen precisely because they are not an expert in all of it — because neither is the reader."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Customized writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/customized-writing/"}]}];

